#== Notice


#Previously, I was a postdoc researcher at EPFL, Switzerland, hosted by [https://www.epfl.ch/labs/lions/people/volkan-cevher/ Prof. Volkan Cevher] from 2021 to 2023. Before that, I spent two years as a postdoc researcher at ESAT-STADIUS, KU Leuven, Belgium, hosted by [https://www.esat.kuleuven.be/sista/members/suykens.html Prof. Johan A.K. Suykens].

#I obtained the Ph.D degree from Institute of Image Processing Pattern Recognition in Department of Automation, Shanghai Jiao Tong University (SJTU) in June 2019, supervised by [http://www.pami.sjtu.edu.cn/En/jieyang Prof. Jie Yang], and the B.E. degree in Automation, Harbin Institute of Technology (HIT) in 2014.

#Here is my [files/CV_Fanghui.pdf CV].


I'm generally interested in *foundations of modern machine learning* from the lens of *learning efficiency*, both theoretically and empirically. My research is always contributing to how to *handle nonlinearity at a theoretical level* and how to precisely and efficiently *approximate nonlinearity at a practical level* under theoretical guidelines, which is a longstanding question over science, technology and engineering.
~~~
{}{img_left}{images/RS_mini.jpg}{alt text}{850px}{220px}
~~~

My research (the past, ongoing and future) focuses on the following directions:
- machine learning theory: what is the largest function space that can be learned by neural networks, both statistically and computationally efficiently (computational-statistical gaps)?
- LLM pertaining: the principles of scaling laws, training dynamics
- post-training in LLMs:  expand the frontiers of empirical and theoretical knowledge on when and where to fine-tune/inference, and how much we can fine-tune/inference, precisely, efficiently, and robustly
- AI4MLT: AI for machine learning theory, lean auto-formalisation

#My research was supported by grants from Royal Society (UK), Alan Turing Institute (UK), and DAAD (Germany).

